{"items":[{"tags":["behavior","discipline","pre-teen","emotional-well-being","lying"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":364,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1767616028,"creation_date":1767258479,"question_id":46844,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46844/how-to-train-young-teen-to-fess-up-and-man-up","title":"How to train young teen to fess up and man up","body":"<p>My 12 year old cannot stand to make mistakes. This has been a theme with him his whole life but at this age when kids make more mistakes, I feel it is a bigger issue. Because he does make mistakes but he lies when he is caught even when caught in the act, even after he has been punished. Since he keeps lying after he has been punished it is not (just) strategic lying to avoid punishment.</p>\n<p>Example: he threw fireworks out our window with a friend. I yelled at them both and send them downstairs to clean it up. In the garden his dad, who took the garden hose to extinguish the smouldering mess, scolded him in front of his friend and spreyed him wet with the hose in the front of our house.\nThis made an impression.</p>\n<p>But still he made up an elaborate story, to make this an accident. He said he picked up some spent fireworks from the street, and put it in his pocket. In his room he took it out and his friend threw put it on the roof. And there it tumbled down and reignited.\nWe know this is untrue: I saw the burning thing fall down as I was looking out, and the friend told his mom that my son had set it on fire and thrown it out.</p>\n<p>Like I said this has been a theme his whole life. In school the teachers that liked him the most would take him apart and try to explain to him that it is OK to make mistakes. They have literally told him this with one of us present so we would confirm it to him. We are not particularly strict. The punishment at our house is not so terrible and it is often absent. We do explain why something is forbidden.</p>\n<p>This has to do with pride. When he was maybe 2.5 or 3, I remember I tried to explain the concept of making up to him after a tantrum. I tried to tell him even grownups can be very angry at each other, but they can make up and then they shake hands. He looked at me in horror, threw himself on the couch and put his hands under his belly to prevent us from shaking hands.</p>\n<p>In the past years things have been going smoothly, so I was not aware we were still dealing with this problem. But now with the lying I think it is very important to teach him that it is OK to make mistakes and to train him to man up to what he does.\nBut how?</p>\n"},{"tags":["screen-time","addiction"],"owner":{"account_id":45333691,"reputation":51,"user_id":67070,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e339cba1036c93a8395adc1d9a0f5772?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Skweaver Weaver Skyler","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67070/skweaver-weaver-skyler"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":304,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1771495258,"creation_date":1768697307,"question_id":46857,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46857/how-do-i-deal-with-poor-screen-discipline-and-lack-of-structure-when-it-comes-to","title":"How do I deal with poor screen discipline and lack of structure when it comes to responsibilies?","body":"<p>I’m really struggling right now because I feel completely undermined as a parent. With my nine year old stepson, we set clear boundaries around chores and screen time, and then they get reversed the moment things get stressful. He cleaned half his room, had a meltdown, and was immediately given screen time after being told he wouldn’t get any so now the room still isn’t clean and the behavior is worse. It feels like teaching him that meltdowns work, and I’m watching him learn that he always gets his way. I don’t mind being the firm one, but I can’t keep doing it alone while his biological parents agrees with me in theory and then caves in practice. It leaves me feeling useless, burnt out, and resentful, like I’m just a chauffeur instead of an actual parent. I warned this would happen years ago with the screen addiction, and now it’s happening, and its bad. I keep warning them this will get worse going into teenagehood. How do I help? What can I do to mediate this situation without feeling like my opinions don't matter? Protesting to his mother doesn't work, but maybe I can approach this in a healthier way when things are calm?</p>\n"},{"tags":["school","violence"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":327,"accepted_answer_id":46861,"answer_count":3,"score":7,"last_activity_date":1771488280,"creation_date":1769296081,"question_id":46859,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46859/teaching-school-aged-children-how-to-handle-conflict-at-school","title":"Teaching school aged children how to handle conflict at school","body":"<p>My kids will be attending an elementary school that's known for behavioral problems and occasional violence. Thinking about this now, I don't know how to approach this type of thing and how to guide them through it / prepare them for it.</p>\n<p>How can I ready them for witnessing or handling violent behavior from other students?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","friends","father","working-parents","moving"],"owner":{"account_id":45435378,"reputation":41,"user_id":67217,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/494c8bce2c5698ffc79b2d4cba8d2800?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Charalampos Papadopoulos","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67217/charalampos-papadopoulos"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":473,"answer_count":3,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1776190747,"creation_date":1769549536,"last_edit_date":1770105300,"question_id":46860,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46860/my-5-year-old-son-doesnt-like-our-new-home","title":"My 5 year old son doesn&#39;t like our new home","body":"<p>I got a permanent position in another city (which is by many people worldwide considered one of the best places to live) a year ago. But people (mainly other parents) here aren't very friendly. My son says he likes more our old home because that's were all his real friends are. He seems very happy all the time, but be says that randomly some days. His teachers tell me that he is also happy at school. Our routine during the day hasn't changed either. Should i approach other parents even if they are not very friendly? Or should i let time pass and give him some time to form close friends?</p>\n"},{"tags":["parents","custody"],"owner":{"account_id":45561606,"reputation":11,"user_id":67405,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/396edb4820d8ef196651a3154103e13b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Caleb B","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67405/caleb-b"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":102,"closed_date":1770618531,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1770591864,"creation_date":1770591864,"question_id":46863,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46863/can-i-temporarily-keep-my-2-year-old-daughter-from-going-with-her-mother","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"Can I temporarily keep my 2 year old daughter from going with her mother?","body":"<p>In the last month my daughter’s mother has wrecked her car while drunk and 2 weeks later was arrested for a DUI in a separate situation. She took my daughter to somebodies house that I don’t know and asked her not too and left her there in the middle of the night in the living room to get drunk. Despite me asking to pick her up earlier that day. Then was arrested for the DUI that night. She lies constantly about my daughters whereabouts. And lives with her grandma having no stable home after we’ve split up in September. Am I right to keep her from taking my daughter today?</p>\n"},{"tags":["stools"],"owner":{"account_id":45645486,"reputation":29,"user_id":67514,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/32aa52001ea522157c2a0a693c8735d1?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ingrid Gillman","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67514/ingrid-gillman"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":667,"answer_count":4,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1788152476,"creation_date":1771346963,"last_edit_date":1771591946,"question_id":46864,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46864/11yr-old-admitted-that-he-poops-his-pants-because-he-doesnt-want-to-miss-anythi","title":"11yr old admitted that he poops his pants because he doesn&#39;t want to miss anything on TV or pause a video game. He also doesn&#39;t think it&#39;s gross. Help","body":"<p>My 11 year old finally admitted that he poops his pants because he doesn't want to miss anything on TV or doesn't' want to pause a video game. He also doesn't think its gross. We need help</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Originally posted as an answer:</p>\n<p>My question is a REAL question. My 11 year old step son really does poop his pants sometimes when he is watching tv or playing video games. Because consequences in general weren’t working, we finally decided that when he’s with us, he doesn’t get TV or video games and that has completely stopped this behavior but at his mom‘s house, he continues to poop his pants because he doesn’t have the same consequences. He is potty trained. He admitted to just being too lazy to get up because he didn’t want to miss anything and he also admitted that he doesn’t think it’s gross. I’ve only been married a year and a half and being a stepmom is a new experience, but I also really truly want to help him with his life skills. I was just curious if anybody else had ever experienced anything like this because to me he is way too old to be having this behavior.</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","primary-schooler","teaching"],"owner":{"account_id":2228586,"reputation":211,"user_id":44819,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c63f6c93d67e8cbe0bc2498c225d2d88?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"MateuszL","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44819/mateuszl"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2687,"accepted_answer_id":46866,"answer_count":3,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1771650066,"creation_date":1771403212,"question_id":46865,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46865/giving-prizes-for-not-being-late-to-school","title":"Giving prizes for not being late to school","body":"<p>TLDR: I plan to start giving prizes for my children for being on time at school - what can possibly go wrong? (it's not as bad I think, bear with me)</p>\n<h2>Situation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>I have 2 children, 7 yo and 9 yo.</li>\n<li>We commute to school. We have option of car or public transport. (Bicycle is an option in better weather, assume it is outside of scope for this question)</li>\n<li>Children prefer car, I prefer public transport (personal reasons)</li>\n<li>Children are often late to school. My attempts at changing it made me feel I am the only person at home who cares about being on time and were generally costly (in terms of time, energy, peace at home vs achieved results)</li>\n<li>Children want to have some collectible cards that are currently popular at school.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What I want to achieve</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>I want children to be on time at school</li>\n<li>I want children to be aware of time (&quot;do I have a time now to do XYZ or do I need to hurry up?&quot;)</li>\n<li>I want us usually to commute by public transport (secondary priority)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How I plan to achieve it</h2>\n<p>I plan to buy some of the cards (30ish for initial roll-out). I will introduce a rule that every day that we go to school with public transport AND are on time, children get 1 card at random (shared or 1 each - undecided yet).</p>\n<p>I think it should be helpful, because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>plan is simple, sufficiently ambitious, achievable.</li>\n<li>even if it doesn't work fully, it will provide incentive in right direction</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Expected problems (please comment on those)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>I fear children could learn that without prize they have NO reason to be on time (main concern)</li>\n<li>If I give them 1 card each, it may appear that 1 is worse than another due to random draw</li>\n<li>If I give them 1 card to share, what happens when 1 is not going to school (e.g. because is sick)?</li>\n<li>It is possible that on a given day only 1 child is difficult, but both share success or failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What are your thoughts? Is this a good idea? Are there problems that I didn't anticipate? How to fix those I did?</p>\n<p>In short: <strong>how to make it work well?</strong></p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","anxiety","autism","depression","biting"],"owner":{"account_id":45669024,"reputation":11,"user_id":67539,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1bcab6e84d72a165c5dec11a6a53861?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ingrid","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67539/ingrid"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":120,"closed_date":1771671511,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1771632431,"creation_date":1771632431,"question_id":46880,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46880/autistic-15year-old-son-stimming-and-social-anxiety","closed_reason":"Needs more focus","title":"Autistic 15year old son, stimming and social anxiety","body":"<p>My Autistic 15year old son, has severe social anxiety. What are some things that you have tried and have helped your kids with social anxiety.\nMy2nd question: Lately,if someone brings up something that causes his stress, he has started to pull his hair and biting his hand. He does wear Chewlery, but what has helped your kids not to self harm?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","primary-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":45703474,"reputation":151,"user_id":67586,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/af581a6ea09748f096b4c3649d6937bf?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Samual Smith","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67586/samual-smith"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1704,"answer_count":5,"score":15,"last_activity_date":1779690271,"creation_date":1771888187,"question_id":46881,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46881/when-playing-games-against-my-kids-such-as-sports-cards-etc-i-always-have-a-ve","title":"When playing games against my kids such as sports cards, etc. I always have a very hard time knowing how to go about playing","body":"<p>Mainly I’m asking in terms of chance and fairness. And I’m referring to playing with my three kids ranging from toddler all the way to eight years old. Should I play my best for example, in backyard basketball, or in the running races to the mailbox, even if that means there is no chance that they would win?</p>\n<p>I’m just using Basketball as an example, because my youngest loves to play with me on the little tykes hoop, and my other two are playing on a lowered goal. I can easily consistently beat them, at the same time I feel guilty putting in an effort to play badly, because even then I am still able to pretty much decide the outcome of the game. I just don’t know if I should play to win, or let them win.</p>\n<p>Another example would be that my toddler loves to have running races with me to the mailbox in the front yard. But she is a toddler and I am a adult, I can walk faster than her fastest run. Even when I put in an effort I’m still able to decide the outcome fairly easily.</p>\n<p>It’s games like this., Games where when it comes down to it they’re just one sided. So should I be letting my kids win, or should I be playing to win?</p>\n"},{"tags":["language","learning"],"owner":{"account_id":5304099,"reputation":131,"user_id":39777,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/lGBOTNx9.png?s=256","display_name":"prab","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39777/prab"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3508,"answer_count":3,"score":13,"last_activity_date":1776077728,"creation_date":1772513274,"question_id":46890,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46890/7-year-old-struggling-with-german-in-germany-limited-family-language-support-h","title":"7-year-old struggling with German in Germany, limited family language support, how can we help effectively?","body":"<p>We live in Germany and have been here for about 10 years. We are originally from India. At home we speak Hindi and English. My wife and I did not actively learn German in the first years because basic German plus English was enough for daily life.</p>\n<p>Now our 7-year-old son is struggling with German at school. His main challenges are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pronunciation</li>\n<li>Dictation and spelling</li>\n<li>Speaking German confidently</li>\n<li>General anxiety or fear around using German</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At home, we have always spoken English and Hindi fluently. We rarely used German with him during his early years. Recently, I started learning German myself, and I noticed that he sometimes sits with me and writes German words. However, he still does not like speaking German with me. I wonder if this is because:</p>\n<p>I sometimes correct my own German mistakes in front of him</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For 7 years, we never expected German from him at home</li>\n<li>He associates German mainly with school pressure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We hired a university student as a tutor. She has a good bond with him. They read, write, and practice pronunciation together, and she also plays with him. That seems helpful, but progress is slow.</p>\n<p>Socially, he mainly interacts with children at school and daycare. We have limited social gatherings, partly due to the weather and partly because our close circle is English-speaking. Most of his free time is spent at home with us, watching TV or playing.</p>\n<p>Recently, I noticed that he prefers playing with younger children rather than peers his age. He sometimes puts in minimal effort to learn the language and hopes to “just pass.” Is it happening due to his slow pace of learning? Is it connected to me, as I am learning German after 10 years, still at an intermediate level?</p>\n<p><strong>His academic performance is starting to be affected. If he does not meet the language requirement, he may not be promoted</strong></p>\n<p>We do not want to pressure him too much and make him dislike studying. At the same time, the situation is becoming serious. <strong>Last year, his class teacher suggested that we repeat class 1 for him, but we believed that repeating a class might demotivate him from learning, as he is also emotional.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>How can we realistically support a 7-year-old in learning the local\nlanguage when the parents are not fluent themselves?</li>\n<li>How can we increase his confidence in speaking German without\nincreasing anxiety?</li>\n<li>Should we introduce more German at home, or is that\ncounterproductive if our own German is imperfect?</li>\n<li>Are there evidence-based strategies for helping children in\nbilingual or late-immersion situations like this?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>We are looking for practical, structured advice that balances academic needs with emotional well-being.</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","discipline","relationships","grandparents","co-parenting"],"owner":{"account_id":45810196,"reputation":57,"user_id":67723,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/58bbfcf3e232035b9c963e14377a3ada?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"MFT mommy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/67723/mft-mommy"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2728,"answer_count":2,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1773199230,"creation_date":1772875240,"last_edit_date":1772876532,"question_id":46894,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46894/is-this-normal-behavior-for-child-and-grandparent","title":"Is this normal behavior for child and grandparent","body":"<p>I have a 4 year old son who shows extremely strong attachment to his grandmother. And in a normal situation I would applaud such connection, however this expresses itself in unhealthy ways. It symptomatically shows up as him crying for grandmother when she’s not around, undermining us parents hence we set a boundary or rule saying grandma does it this way, or if grandma is around child runs to her for comfort or to escape consequence.</p>\n<p><em>a little background I lived with my mother while he was a baby and I was finishing graduate school. This included long hours of studying and internship where she was the main caregiver, however the last 2 years I have been consistently back and involved in his life. Grandma only watches him one day a week and he attends preschool 3 half days (that is when I get a majority of my work done).</em></p>\n<p>The main issue I encounter is that grandma has a different permissive parenting style where our rules as parents is overridden constantly. This can be anything from food, nap times, behavior/etiquette/manners to spoiling with toys and outings. He is not reprimanded for yelling or demanding things while I am urging him to be polite. I also believe in sleep training and that he should sleep in his own bedroom. For this, I am shamed for exercising an authoritative parenting style being called “abusive” and “cruel”. The worst part is that we still live with grandma (because building my practice has been a slow and painful process due to her lack of support and demands extra funds for “babysitting”) and he refuses to sleep in his room unless she is with him or she constantly tries to bring him in her room to sleep with her. She definitely has some major narcissistic traits and I am noticing co-dependency issues with my son..</p>\n<p>Thank God me and my partner just bought a house and will be moved in by the end of the month, but grandma is acting like it’s the end of the world (after all she’s done and sacrificed, everyone is abandoning her) and is adamant that we stop sending him to preschool and that she watch him instead..</p>\n<p>Honestly, I think she’s a malignant narcissist and is using my son to stroke her ego and create a power play since I’ve wised up to her gaslighting antics and don’t believe a word she says.</p>\n<p>But naturally I want to gather a few outside opinions and see what other parents feel. This will decide If we completely burn the bridge and cut off grandma entirely, or if we allow her to visit occasionally but only with us all present. It sounds horrible cutting her off entirely, but I’m feeling like we have no other choice since she refuses to respect my parenting rules and choices and co-parent responsibly.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","pre-teen","emotional-well-being","family"],"owner":{"account_id":3690277,"reputation":741,"user_id":39807,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/WREQP.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ben","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39807/ben"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":559,"accepted_answer_id":46903,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1775759708,"creation_date":1773973814,"question_id":46902,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46902/my-youngests-new-appearance-is-triggering-based-on-the-reputation-of-the-eldes","title":"My youngest&#39;s new appearance is triggering, based on the reputation of the eldest","body":"<p>So, that title is not overly explanatory. To expand:</p>\n<p>My eldest son has created an incredibly bad reputation for himself over the past 2 years. He had started skipping classes, leaving school grounds, was almost expelled for joining in with his friends that were throwing a large hunting knife at a tree on school grounds, and was finally pulled out of the school due to accusations of sexual harassment of another student (he-said-she-said situation, but profiling was obviously swayed against him). We have transferred him to a new school, which is far more strict and responsive to truancy, yet he is still choosing to skip class (leaving school grounds is a lot harder).</p>\n<p>He has also become more emotional/depressed, and has withdrawn from us for help - opting to talk to his aunts/uncles, who then immediately refer him back to us, or fill us in so that we can understand and approach the situation ourselves. This behavior has also involved scratching himself with blades like pencil sharpeners (he has not cut himself - it's only skin deep - it has become apparent this is very much for attention; he does not have a tolerance for pain).</p>\n<p>The last part is obviously more normal for a 14/15 year old, however, regardless of our efforts, he still chooses to reject us and our help. We have also been taking him to therapy, which he has accepted and engaged in, but we are still overcoming the fallout of the behavior.</p>\n<p>Now, my youngest son has adopted a similar appearance with his hair - wearing it exactly the same way as his older brother (combed down over his face to hide his eyes). He has said &quot;it's just how I like it&quot;, which I can understand. Personally, I don't think it looks any good - I had similar conversations with my other son when he started doing it.</p>\n<p>The main issue however is that it has triggered some strong emotions in me just by seeing it; it brings up the stress and struggles that we have been through with his older brother. I'm concerned that he is copying his brother's appearance, because he idolizes him, or that he is in a similar situation at his school (we put him in a different school to his older brother due to the issues we were facing there). I obviously don't want him to fall into the same patterns and behaviors as dealing with that once had a severe impact on everyone in the family.</p>\n<p>I'm at a stand still on how to approach this - I don't want to suppress my son's self-expression of who he is, but I also can't ignore how I am feeling, why I am feeling the way I do - the cause and potential impact. I know that dealing with it is something I need to do myself apart from this, but I do also want to express my concerns and help them (potentially both) to understand why I feel the way I do.</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","adolescent"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1263,"accepted_answer_id":46911,"answer_count":3,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1776152944,"creation_date":1775773516,"last_edit_date":1776097292,"question_id":46907,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46907/are-there-any-issues-with-trading-responsibilities-for-privileges","title":"Are there any issues with trading responsibilities for privileges?","body":"<p>My wife and I have used a positive discipline approach for the majority of our children's lives. We don't really punish them, when they do something silly or anti-social we use it as an opportunity to explain better behavior. Basically we don't make them feel bad, we explain and model what the appropriate behavior is. This approach has been pretty successful, they both have strong self-regulation and social skills now.</p>\n<p>But now we seem to be in a new era where they know they can break a rule and nothing bad will happen. I'm not too worried about that, but I feel like we're moving into new territory where there needs to be firmer repercussions for their actions.</p>\n<p>I've already thought about this, and even asked a similar question a few years ago. The only approach that makes sense to me (that aligns with positive discipline) is to explain to them that they have certain responsibilities that they have to fulfill, and if they don't fulfill those responsibilities then there may be a loss of privileges.</p>\n<p>For example, 'your responsibility is to keep your glasses safe', if they end up broken we're going to have to use some of your allowance to pay for the replacement. We don't say it in a harsh or demeaning way, we just explain the reality to them and let the consequence be the consequence. Maybe they screw up and we give them a little bit of leeway to build rapport.</p>\n<p>At this time our boys are 4 and 6, although I used the adolescent tag because I'm interested in answers that span the rest of their childhood.</p>\n<p>My question is: is this approach basically sound? I can't really envision any other way for positive discipline to work once they're out of the baby stage. And are there any gotchas or alternative approaches that might be worth considering?</p>\n"},{"tags":["parents"],"owner":{"account_id":46060113,"reputation":99,"user_id":68026,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/9QCdu4hK.png?s=256","display_name":"Tony","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68026/tony"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2587,"answer_count":2,"score":8,"last_activity_date":1776773172,"creation_date":1776088848,"question_id":46913,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46913/is-there-a-way-to-get-alerts-when-my-daughter-receives-calls-from-numbers-not-sa","title":"Is there a way to get alerts when my daughter receives calls from numbers not saved in her contacts?","body":"<p>I’m trying to find a simple way to be notified if my daughter gets a call from an unknown number. I checked the phone’s settings but didn’t see anything that clearly does this. I don’t want to constantly check her phone, just get an alert when something unfamiliar comes up. I search on google and chatgpt for the answer but did not find anything that really solves this issue.</p>\n<p>My main concern is I don't want my child to be in contact with wrong people or feel uncomfortable dealing with someone she doesn’t know.</p>\n"},{"tags":["parents","emotional-well-being","abuse"],"owner":{"account_id":12252380,"reputation":600,"user_id":32209,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6fec1a984ec0320a83126667eff3a0e7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"suse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32209/suse"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2608,"accepted_answer_id":46921,"answer_count":1,"score":11,"last_activity_date":1776376165,"creation_date":1776225351,"question_id":46919,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46919/is-it-considered-abuse-when-a-parent-demands-that-a-young-child-give-him-a-hug","title":"Is it considered abuse when a parent demands that a young child give him a hug?","body":"<p>One parent of a two-parent otherwise-normal family frequently demands that his children give him a hug and when the children (ages 3-6) do not want to ( because they are playing or busy doing something else or just don't feel like it), he pretends to cry and then they come running to hug or kiss him. Is this considered a form of abuse?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","crying","fears","anxiety"],"owner":{"account_id":46150459,"reputation":11,"user_id":68134,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cd0caf70e41b36697695c1c933f1cd43?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Armando Salas","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68134/armando-salas"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":96,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1776522511,"creation_date":1776522511,"question_id":46923,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46923/2-5-year-old-son-gets-anxious-and-scratches-ears-then-head-and-face-once-scared","title":"2.5 year old son gets anxious and scratches ears then head and face once scared","body":"<p>It began when a fly flew past his ear close enough to so he heard it and it really made him uncomfortable and scared. I believe it was similar to hair clippers which he also has difficulty with. Now at seemingly random times he’ll begin scrapes ear as if he’s keeping the fly away and slowly ramps up to full on crying and screaming about “a fly” even if one’s not around. It’s almost paranoid.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","literacy"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":414,"accepted_answer_id":46931,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1785864702,"creation_date":1776850264,"last_edit_date":1777336026,"question_id":46925,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46925/is-there-a-recommended-method-of-introducing-letter-sounds-to-a-four-year-old","title":"Is there a recommended method of introducing letter sounds to a four year old?","body":"<p>I've been thinking about literacy concepts for one of my children and trying to understand how we can continue to broaden his horizons. At this point I'm of the opinion that it's too early to push it, but we do read to him a lot, have introduced letter concepts (mostly with his name), and have had him trace letters in workbooks from time to time. He doesn't seem to have a firm grasp of what letters are yet (which I figure is age appropriate). We're keeping it casual at this age because I don't think pressure is going to do anything positive for him.</p>\n<p>All of that being said, are there appropriate, recommended guidelines for introducing letters and letter sounds at this age? If it's too early (he just turned four), at what point would it be appropriate to to introduce a minimal amount of structure? And what would that structure look like?</p>\n<p>ETA: I'm wondering if this question may be too difficult to answer. I've been researching the topic for a few weeks and am struggling to find anything like a recommended framework. I'm mostly getting various opinions. Some recommend focusing on letter sounds and not names, others recommend focusing on names as well.</p>\n<p>From what I can gather the more critical part is going to be introducing these concepts to our son while keeping things fun and not pressuring him. Being encouraging and not too rigid.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen"],"owner":{"account_id":46225673,"reputation":41,"user_id":68225,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/faa0528679cd900a2668e5f9fdeb548c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Adeline Stock","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68225/adeline-stock"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":215,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1779369882,"creation_date":1777387246,"question_id":46927,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46927/how-do-i-move-forward-with-my-overbearing-mom","title":"How do I move forward with my overbearing mom?","body":"<p>I'm 15 and feel super trapped. My mom tracks me at all times, randomly goes through my phone, and searches my room and bed while I'm in it.</p>\n<p>The tracking I understand because she wants me to be safe, but whenever the app is wrong it's my fault for not being where I'm supposed to be even if I am.</p>\n<p>I also understand that as a teenager digital privacy isn't something I get the privilege of, but I think that if my parents have decided to trust me with a phone then they should embrace it. I have zero access to the internet and very limited apps with no social media. My contacts are constantly monitored and viewed by her and have to be kept up to her standards. Whenever I do something &quot;wrong&quot; my phone is the first thing to go. It has super strict time restrictions and even in the allowed time I have to use it (7am-9pm) it will get taken throughout the day. She has rules like no phones in rooms and they're only allowed in public spaces, but our backyard doesn't count and all of my calls are listened to.</p>\n<p>She has searched my bed multiple times and many while I was in it. This one time it was around 2am and she was super convinced that I had my phone in my bed and proceeded to pat me down,turn on all of the lights, throw back the sheets, and turn over my pillows. My phone had been turned in on the family desk where it was supposed to be, but she couldn't find it and immediately jumped to the worst case scenario.</p>\n<p>Whenever she gets a feeling that I've done something wrong she'll check my phone and inevitably find something she doesn't like. How do I tell her that if she keeps looking for it she's gonna find it?</p>\n<p>This isn't even all of it, she hates my boyfriend and is convinced that all of my friends are keeping stuff about me from her. I've never failed a drug test, I'm in band, getting straight A's, and am thinking about colleges and my PhD. I don't know what else she wants from me. I have a relatively good relationship with my mom on good days and my dad couldn't care less about my phone or boyfriend. He's had to warn me when she comes home and my boyfriend's over and has had to physically stop her from lashing out at me.</p>\n<p>I am very grateful that I have 2 parents who care about me, but she's so overbearing and I can't make mistakes or learn from them without it being constantly monitored or intervened on.</p>\n"},{"tags":["communication","pre-teen","conflict"],"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3474,"answer_count":5,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1790090466,"creation_date":1779231980,"question_id":46935,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46935/how-to-talk-to-10-yo-about-argument-between-me-and-wife","title":"How to talk to 10 yo about argument between me and wife","body":"<p>So whenever we are in car and I discuss something with my wife, mostly it end up being an argument and I stop the conversation by saying “leave it”. I say it for the sake of kids sitting behind. Now this has happened a lot and I feel like my son might be thinking something or wanting answers?</p>\n<p>How can I talk to 10 yo about the argument we have and how he sees it or have questions?</p>\n<p>In car we have 10 and 6 yo.</p>\n"},{"tags":["step-parents"],"owner":{"account_id":46395903,"reputation":11,"user_id":68448,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/dfd6550b74d3fdaf797d162f99219bf3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Hazel Phillips","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68448/hazel-phillips"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":145,"closed_date":1779818637,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1779549591,"creation_date":1779512474,"question_id":46941,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46941/why-does-my-step-mom-get-mad-at-me-for-every-little-thing-i-do","closed_reason":"Opinion-based","title":"Why does my step mom get mad at me for every little thing i do?","body":"<p>I'm a 16 year old living with my step mom, dad and grandma. I can do everything they want me to do but the second I mess up on something or get an 'attitude' I'm seen as she problem and get called lazy for it. Just recently I was going put my computer up as my grandma was saying put it back so I became the problem. I'm starting to get tired of this treatment but I have no where else to go. What do I do?</p>\n"},{"tags":["motivation","reward"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2306,"accepted_answer_id":46949,"answer_count":3,"score":8,"last_activity_date":1780561100,"creation_date":1780425764,"question_id":46947,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46947/can-it-make-sense-to-pay-a-child-to-learn-a-skill","title":"Can it make sense to pay a child to learn a skill?","body":"<p>I'll caveat this question with the fact that my family is very much opposed to rewards and positive reinforcement. We try to praise only the effort and never tie financial incentives to anything, especially results.</p>\n<p>That being said we've discovered that the elementary school our children are attending no longer teach the students to touch type on a keyboard, and that is absolutely a skill that we want them to have. Being able to type quickly on a real keyboard will have huge returns for them over the course of their lifetimes.</p>\n<p>Starting with the assumption that we start out teaching them to type with no financial incentive at all and see how it goes / how their intrinsic motivation is, and the second assumption that if they need extrinsic motivation (payment) we don't tie that to results, but just say - you practice for 30 minutes and we'll give you (for example) 5 dollars, and we explain to them why we're doing it this way (typing is important). Could that work? Do you see any downsides to this approach?</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler","reading"],"owner":{"account_id":6492099,"reputation":119,"user_id":68704,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/95f4e02eca6b32a6870b387bb1e8a66c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"ool","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68704/ool"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":179,"accepted_answer_id":46958,"answer_count":2,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1785745479,"creation_date":1783597300,"question_id":46957,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46957/word-lists-dolch-20095-and-fry-200-used-up-to-which-grades-in-usa-schools","title":"Word lists: Dolch 200+95 and Fry 200 - used up to which grades in USA schools?","body":"<p>Edward Dolch's list of 220 non-nouns plus 95 nouns (gathered from three other lists in 1936) is used up to which grade in USA schools? And what about Edward Fry's &quot;200&quot; list, meaning his first two lists each of 100 words - what grade are they used up to?</p>\n<p>I am interested in the grades at which school pupils in the USA are expected to be able to read all of the Dolch 220+95 and all of the Fry 200. I couldn't find a good source with this info. I realise the ages probably vary, but I'm just looking for a rough answer, which I think may possibly be 3rd grade (D 220+95) and 2nd grade (F 200).</p>\n"},{"tags":["middle-childhood"],"owner":{"account_id":3091856,"reputation":672,"user_id":11975,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":11,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2603d58bdf44e284de21ed1c1f416d25?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user2617804","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11975/user2617804"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":147,"answer_count":0,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1784365782,"creation_date":1784196499,"last_edit_date":1784365782,"question_id":46960,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46960/what-do-about-my-son-who-is-best-friends-with-a-largely-unsupervised-bad-boy","title":"What do about my son who is best friends with a largely unsupervised bad boy?","body":"<p>My son is in 5th grade. He has lots of friends but his best friend is one whose parents are largely nonpresent. His best friend is slighly bad such as handling dangerous items such as chisels.This is Japan- it is illegal to carry such things and anyway they do nothing but bang things together not make things. The child was banned from afterschool care (at his school) from violent behaviour. We don't want him to go his friend's house because his parents or other adults are rarely present. We both work and it is school holidays and we are poor. What can we do? My wife is opposed to setting clear written house rules. We now have started to have house rules without clear punishments and some rules are rather unreasonable like home by 4:30 pm when he get home at 4pm and it is Summer.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","primary-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":46823304,"reputation":27,"user_id":68823,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/df5b758337735d43d7c1460441bd439e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"LillyBethanny","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68823/lillybethanny"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":323,"closed_date":1787856650,"answer_count":1,"score":-3,"last_activity_date":1786998301,"creation_date":1785689242,"question_id":46964,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46964/how-do-you-play-with-your-young-kids-why-not-play-correctly","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"How do you play with your young kids? Why not play correctly?","body":"<p>I have noticed that when parents play with their young kids, for some reason they won’t genuinely play. Why is this?</p>\n<p>For example If a child wants to arm wrestle or perhaps play fight with them the adult seemingly barely even does much of anything, and it is very obvious that they are just straight up letting their child win. Why doesn’t the adult actually put in effort?</p>\n<p>another example if their child wants to have a running race with them.  Why don’t the parents actually run? Instead they will sort of do this really awkward bending while walking, and they again end up just letting their child win.</p>\n<p>If you were to play fight, arm wrestle, or have a running race with your friend, you would actually try, and actually play. Playing with your friend is no different. So I don’t parents actually put an effort and play these correctly With their children</p>\n<p>Anyways, I’ve always been curious, is there a reason that parents don’t play these activities correctly when playing with their toddler or young child?</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":46823304,"reputation":27,"user_id":68823,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/df5b758337735d43d7c1460441bd439e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"LillyBethanny","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68823/lillybethanny"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":171,"closed_date":1787856698,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1785836497,"creation_date":1785695068,"question_id":46965,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46965/how-close-are-me-and-my-cousin-athletically","closed_reason":"Opinion-based","title":"How close are me and my cousin athletically?","body":"<p>I will be visiting my 6 year old cousin soon and her mom said that lately she has been in the competitive mode and is really excited for me to come over. Over the phone Lily told me that she really wants to do things like race, play fight, etc. I am 17 and honestly I’m only around others my age, she lives far away so I don’t see her very often. I know she is shorter than me but our kids her age pretty fast and pretty strong? I’m very athletic myself but still, I’m just trying to get an idea of what to expect when we compete.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":42038630,"reputation":9,"user_id":68849,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d096106753d7f715f64d9f156cfc804a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Poke","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68849/poke"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":339,"answer_count":3,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1790720271,"creation_date":1786031441,"last_edit_date":1790720271,"question_id":46971,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46971/what-age-do-boys-finally-learn-to-aim-in-toilet","title":"What age do boys finally learn to aim in toilet?","body":"<p>My boys are 13 and 15 and still end up getting urine all over the toilet seat, floor, and even the walls. I am so tired of having to clean it up.</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","discipline","parents","age-appropriate","relationships"],"owner":{"account_id":46876108,"reputation":1,"user_id":68876,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/719aea2ba19e85b79d6110e0187d3014?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user68876","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68876/user68876"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":466,"answer_count":3,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1787838293,"creation_date":1786472570,"question_id":46975,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46975/would-i-be-overreacting-if-i-banned-my-daughter-from-reading-alex-rider-comics-o","title":"Would I be overreacting if I banned my daughter from reading Alex Rider comics online?","body":"<p>I walked in on my 11-year-old daughter reading a comic online and this image was one of the comic pages. The comic is called &quot;Alex Rider Eagle Strike&quot;. I looked into this and the naked character is 14. I am considering banning my daughter from reading these comics. Would I be overreacting or is this the correct choice?</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/K69MV2Gy.jpg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/K69MV2Gy.jpg\" alt=\"enter image description here\" /></a></p>\n"},{"tags":["parents","middle-childhood","cellphone"],"owner":{"account_id":46913382,"reputation":91,"user_id":68916,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XWgnybic.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Hiterd hojery","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68916/hiterd-hojery"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":4534,"answer_count":8,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1790087353,"creation_date":1787049538,"question_id":46984,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46984/chaos-inside-my-home-due-to-mobile-phones","title":"Chaos inside my home due to mobile phones","body":"<p>My son is 9 years old and throws a wild tantrum if we forbid him from using the mobile phone. I don’t want him to use the mobile phone for a very long time because he’s at a very impressionable age. He’s becoming tech-savvy and knows his way around the device. I worry that he might come across and watch inappropriate content on the internet.</p>\n<p>Any advice on how to deal with this situation? I’d appreciate all the do’s and don’ts.</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","pre-teen","money"],"owner":{"account_id":18493691,"reputation":271,"user_id":68977,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/j6ZxI.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Sursula","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/68977/sursula"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3956,"answer_count":5,"score":17,"last_activity_date":1787758214,"creation_date":1787558344,"question_id":46990,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46990/11-year-old-asked-random-people-for-money-to-buy-fish-for-his-new-aquarium-how","title":"11 year old asked random people for money to buy fish for his new aquarium - how to teach him that this is not ok?","body":"<p>My kid (11) and partner went to an event this weekend and when they came home, my partner told me that during this event, our kid apparently started asking people for money to fund fish for his new aquarium. Apparently, he got close to 80€. And my partner only became aware after around 40€ were already collected, and was baffled by this and did not really know how to deal with it, so it went on. Apparently he (the kid) was not pushy or anything, just his normal charming self (unlike me, kid is a people person). Most people at the event were either friends, friends of friends or acquaintances.</p>\n<p>A bit of background: the birthday was 2 weeks and we gifted him an aquarium (which does not exist yet as we were away too much for the summer to really start with the process).</p>\n<p>I am a bit mortified that he did that, as, while not rich are sufficiently well off to pay for all the fish we want, albeit we said that we are not going to buy the most expensive fish due to this being the first aquarium he ever had, and also because I want him to know that one does not have to always buy the most expensive stuff. So what probably happend is that he got money from people that might not be as well off as we are. I also wonder what these people will now think of us, because our kid runs around &quot;begging&quot; for money.</p>\n<p>This is the first time anything like this ever happened, and if I could I would give the money back, but I (and neither my partner) don't know from whom the money came from. But I definitely do not want this to happen again. What are good ways to deal with something like this? Should we donate the money for a good cause? I feel like if he gets to keep it, there is little to be learned.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":439169,"reputation":563,"user_id":29041,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ffb6ad39ce95d58890d2e7ea2cb72a04?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Erel Segal-Halevi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29041/erel-segal-halevi"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3028,"answer_count":2,"score":14,"last_activity_date":1787842880,"creation_date":1787609599,"question_id":46991,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/46991/my-son-is-in-a-kindergarten-with-much-younger-kids-what-are-my-options","title":"My son is in a kindergarten with much younger kids - what are my options?","body":"<p>My son is 4 years old. In my town, there are only two kindergartens for children 3-6 years old. So 3-4 yo children go to kindergarten 1 and 5-6 yo children go to kindergarten 2, and the 4-5 yo children are split between the two by their age. My son went to kindergarten 1 last year, and this year he was assigned to the same kindergarten.</p>\n<p>Most children in kindergarten 1 are much younger than him, both biologically and mentally. He says he is very bored and does not have friends to play with (most friends he had last year moved to kindergarten 2). Also, the stories and materials taught in the kindergarten are the same as last year, which adds to his boredom. He tells me each day &quot;I do not want to go to kindergarten&quot;. Yesterday he told me &quot;today I wanted to run away from the kindergarten, as I was tired from the plays, but I overcame myself&quot;.</p>\n<p>I asked the managers several times to move him to kindergarten 2, but they said it is not possible due to capacity constraints. The ideal solution would be to open kindergarten 3 for children 4-5 years old, but this is not possible due to the town budget constraints.</p>\n<p>I tried to encourage my son to behave as a &quot;big brother&quot; to the younger kids: teach them things that he knows, help them play the games, etc., but he did not seem very interested in this.</p>\n<p>What other options do I have, that would make the kindergarten visits more enjoyable and meaningful for my son?</p>\n"},{"tags":["safety","cosleeping","baby"],"owner":{"account_id":266065,"reputation":1400,"user_id":6775,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/onUUf.jpg?s=256","display_name":"rumtscho","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6775/rumtscho"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":146,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1787914685,"creation_date":1787822155,"question_id":47001,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/47001/how-do-i-know-when-babies-can-go-over-bed-guards","title":"How do I know when babies can go over bed guards?","body":"<p>My twin babies (9.5 months old) sleep in my bed. I have placed a mesh bed guard (<a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/B0FFSH3C96\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this type</a>) on all four sides, screwed into the bed frame.</p>\n<p>Two nights ago, they discovered a new game: climbing up the guards. They grab the upper edge and use a combination of pull-ups and bracing their feet against the mesh to go up. They can now go at least as far up as to bite the edge from above (I see the spit marks), and the feet come up almost 20 cm above the mattress.</p>\n<p>On the one hand, I want to think that they are still safe. They don't the upper body strength to do a very high pull up, and bracing the legs becomes more difficult the higher the legs are. I don't think I could climb over a scaled-up guard myself (although maybe I can? I don't have the opportunity to test). And the official wisdom is that these guards are safe until they reach to the baby's armpits (currently, the babies are 72-ish cm tall and the rails are 67).</p>\n<p>On the other hand, I still feel quite worried when I see them go that high after only two evenings of exercising. They are very advanced in motor development. Also, they are underweight (around 7.5 kg), which seems to help them in any activity where their muscles have to carry their weight.</p>\n<p>How do I recognize when they are advanced enough to be able to climb over the guard?</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","parents","relationships","dating"],"owner":{"account_id":46988621,"reputation":29,"user_id":69033,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9b3b7205ee01b05e4b115bb31ded58b2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Skijlyn Zohner","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/69033/skijlyn-zohner"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":156,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1788276683,"creation_date":1788212365,"last_edit_date":1788276683,"question_id":47009,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/47009/how-do-i-tell-my-mom-and-dad-that-i-like-a-boy","title":"How do I tell my mom and dad that I like a boy?","body":"<p>Me and this boy have hugged multiple times (so we both like each other) but his parents don't like him dating I guess. And I don't want to tell my mom that I feel like I can't talk to  her without being scared of her and talk to her about a stupid thing like a boy.</p>\n<p>We have both only known of each other for a little over a week but we both agreed we think each other is cute and we both wanna date  but we're scared of our parents. The last time I told my parents I was dating a boy and they made fun of him and wanted me to break up with him but I really liked him. so I guess I'm scared that will happen again.</p>\n<p>Me and this new boy named Cooper are really good at  being friends and I'm also scared that us dating will ruin the friendship. Every time we hug at school he hugs over my backpack (that's a gentleman move) which I really liked. The one time he didn't he hugged up higher which is still good. Hugging my lower back kinda sends the &quot;I just want to hook up and I only think your hot. nothing else.&quot; signal which I REALLY don't get from this boy. I think he likes me for myself.</p>\n<p>Plus he is TOTALLY my type, orange almost brown hair, freckles, blue eyes and I don't know what it is about his nose but I really like it. I really like him and I don't want to stop cause my parents don't want me dating but they also don't have a problem with it I guess.</p>\n"},{"tags":["newborn"],"owner":{"account_id":897451,"reputation":469,"user_id":17171,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4RbRV.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Neil Meyer","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17171/neil-meyer"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":212,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1791955814,"creation_date":1789059108,"question_id":47011,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/47011/how-do-you-explain-your-strong-desire-to-have-the-baby-sleep-with-you-to-your-wi","title":"How do you explain your strong desire to have the baby sleep with you to your wife?","body":"<p>I have the strong desire to house our infants sleeping arrangements in the master bedroom where me and my wife sleep. I'm not talking about in the bed, in a cot next to the bed. I don't exactly know how to explain the reasons why into words. I just have the strong desire to have the baby close to me when I sleep.</p>\n<p>Off course I'm not talking about teenagers. I'm Ok for the child to go to his own space as he grows into a toddler, but for the first little while I have the need to have the baby close to me when I sleep. Is this desire natural?</p>\n<p>I hope I'm not being selfish and I hope I could discuss this with my wife in an easy way. I keep on seeing people talk about baby monitors for when the baby start crying, but I cannot do it like that. If the baby cries I need to know about it because he should be close to me. I don't know if other people have felt like this?</p>\n<p>This has awaken something quite visceral or primal in me. I feel the strong urge to have the baby close to me for about the first year of it's life. I have spent my life in a series of rational academic pursuits but now that parenting is possible I seem to have awaken the lizard part of my brain. I hope some people could relate. I feel like I'm a Vulcan experiencing Pon Farr for the first time.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","computer","internet"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":149,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1790930350,"creation_date":1790776365,"question_id":47020,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/47020/tell-a-teen-why-private-browsing-is-turned-off-yes-or-no","title":"Tell a teen why Private Browsing is turned off, yes or no?","body":"<p>Is it better to be completely upfront and tell a teenager (13) I changed the browser settings on their laptop to make it possible to keep track of their online activities, or to handle this in another way?\nFor example by changing the settings quietly or even not changing the settings and keep track of their online activities in some other way?</p>\n<p>The first option is honest but risks creating a conflict for nothing. The second option bypasses conflict, but is sneaky and not the kind of example I want to set.</p>\n<p>Other options like periodic history checks are a lot more hassle and again beg the question: out in the open or after hours?</p>\n<p>Related questions <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/2750/should-i-monitor-my-childs-internet-usage\">here</a> and <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/32712/teenagers-and-internet-access\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>Technical background:\nMy child has a Macbook (for school) with Screen Time and I have parental controls with my iPad. When he uses private browsing I <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/ios/comments/1iyp8zb/private_browsing_tracking_in_screen_time/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">cannot see</a> which websites he visited.</p>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":283}